From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 01:05:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] luma: windows travel to and from their dock icon

Minimizing a window draws it into its app's dock icon, and restoring draws
it back out. An app opened from an icon, in the dock or in search, grows its
first window out of that icon. Any other window grows in gently from 94% of
its size. Closing never travels: the window settles to 95% and fades in
place in 180ms. Minimize and restore take 320ms, and opening takes 280ms.

The motion transforms only the window actor (scale and translation) on one
Clutter timeline, with no offscreen buffer or shader, so its cost does not
grow with the window's size. Its path (lumaWindowPath.js, unit-tested) is
shaped like a lamp's light going back into it. Along the shelf the window
gathers early and toward the shelf it follows later, so it narrows as it
leaves and pours into the icon. The two axes swap for a left or right
shelf. The icon is the dash's own geometry for the window's app. The
fallbacks are the dock island's centre, then the middle of the shelf's
edge on the window's monitor.

A restore that cuts a minimize short, or the other way round, reverses from
where the window had got to instead of jumping. Reduced motion completes
every change at once, and GNOME's slow-down setting applies. A window that
Tiling Shell's window memory holds until it is placed still takes its launch
icon at map time, so the icon cannot attach to a later window.
WindowManager.revealWindow() plays the same opening when such a window is
shown.

The minimize and close shapes were informed by GNOME's upstream minimize to
icon geometry and by the idea of the Compiz magic lamp. No code was taken
from any extension.

Dropping this patch restores the previous animations.
